.TH numfmt 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.\" Define monospaced roman font for groff in troff mode.
.if t .if \n(.g \{\
. ds mono \f(CR
. ds /mono \fP
.\}
.SH NAME
numfmt \- Convert numbers from/to human-readable strings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY numfmt
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.RI [ NUMBER \~.\|.\|.\&]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Reformat NUMBER(s), or the numbers from standard input if none are specified.
.PP
Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-debug
\fB\-\-debug\fP
.UE
print warnings about invalid input
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-d
\fB\-d, \-\-delimiter=\,X\fP
.UE
use X instead of whitespace for field delimiter
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-field
\fB\-\-field=FIELDS\fP
.UE
replace the numbers in these input fields (default=1);
see FIELDS below for details
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-format
\fB\-\-format=FORMAT\fP
.UE
use printf style floating\-point FORMAT;
see FORMAT below for details
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-from
\fB\-\-from=UNIT\fP
.UE
auto\-scale input numbers to UNITs; default is 'none';
see UNIT below for details
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-from\-unit
\fB\-\-from\-unit=N\fP
.UE
specify the input unit size (instead of the default 1)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-grouping
\fB\-\-grouping\fP
.UE
use locale\-defined grouping of digits, e.g. 1,000,000.
This has no effect in the C/POSIX locale
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-header
\fB\-\-header[=N]\fP
.UE
print (without converting) the first N header lines;
N defaults to 1 if not specified
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-invalid
\fB\-\-invalid=MODE\fP
.UE
failure mode for invalid numbers;
MODE can be: abort (default), fail, warn, ignore
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-padding
\fB\-\-padding=N\fP
.UE
pad the output to N characters;
positive N will right\-align, negative N will left\-align;
padding is ignored if the output is wider than N;
the default is to automatically pad if a whitespace is found
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-round
\fB\-\-round=METHOD\fP
.UE
use METHOD for rounding when scaling; METHOD can be:
up, down, from\-zero (default), towards\-zero, nearest
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-suffix
\fB\-\-suffix=SUFFIX\fP
.UE
add SUFFIX to output numbers,
and accept an optional SUFFIX in input numbers
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-unit\-separator
\fB\-\-unit\-separator=SEP\fP
.UE
insert SEP between number and unit on output,
and accept an optional SEP in input numbers
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-to
\fB\-\-to=UNIT\fP
.UE
auto\-scale output numbers to UNITs; see UNIT below
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-\-to\-unit
\fB\-\-to\-unit=N\fP
.UE
the output unit size (instead of the default 1)
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#numfmt\-z
\fB\-z, \-\-zero\-terminated\fP
.UE
line delimiter is NUL, not newline
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/numfmt#numfmt\-\-help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/numfmt#numfmt\-\-version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.SS "UNIT options:"
.TP
none
no auto\-scaling is done; suffixes will trigger an error
.TP
auto
accept optional single/two letter suffix:
1K = 1000, 1k = 1000,
1Ki = 1024,
1M = 1000000,
1Mi = 1048576,
.TP
si
accept optional single letter suffix:
1k = 1000, 1K = 1000,
1M = 1000000,
\&...
.TP
iec
accept optional single letter suffix:
1K = 1024, 1k = 1024,
1M = 1048576,
\&...
.TP
iec\-i
accept optional two\-letter suffix:
1Ki = 1024, 1ki = 1024,
1Mi = 1048576,
\&...
.SS "FIELDS supports \fBcut\fP(1) style field ranges:"
.TP
N
N'th field, counted from 1
.TP
N\-
from N'th field, to end of line
.TP
N\-M
from N'th to M'th field (inclusive)
.TP
\fB\-M\fR
from first to M'th field (inclusive)
.TP
\-
all fields
.PP
Multiple fields/ranges can be separated with commas
.PP
FORMAT must be suitable for printing one floating\-point argument '%f'.
Optional quote (%'f) will enable \fB\-\-grouping\fR (if supported by current locale).
Optional width value (%10f) will pad output. Optional zero (%010f) width
will zero pad the number. Optional negative values (%\-10f) will left align.
Optional precision (%.1f) will override the input determined precision.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
if all input numbers were successfully converted.
.TP
2
if a conversion error occurred.
.IP
By default,
.B numfmt
will stop at the first conversion error.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-invalid=\[aq]fail\[aq] ,
every conversion error is diagnosed,
and the exit status is 2.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-invalid=\[aq]warn\[aq] ,
every conversion error is diagnosed,
but the exit status is 0.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-invalid=\[aq]ignore\[aq] ,
conversion errors are not diagnosed,
and the exit status is 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
\*[mono]$ numfmt --to=si 1000\*[/mono]
.IP
\-> "1.0k"
.IP
\*[mono]$ numfmt --to=iec 2048\*[/mono]
.IP
\-> "2.0K"
.IP
\*[mono]$ numfmt --to=iec-i 4096\*[/mono]
.IP
\-> "4.0Ki"
.IP
\*[mono]$ echo 1K | numfmt --from=si\*[/mono]
.IP
\-> "1000"
.IP
\*[mono]$ echo 1K | numfmt --from=iec\*[/mono]
.IP
\-> "1024"
.IP
\*[mono]$ df -B1 | numfmt --header --field 2-4 --to=si\*[/mono]
.br
\*[mono]$ ls -l  | numfmt --header --field 5 --to=iec\*[/mono]
.br
\*[mono]$ ls -lh | numfmt --header --field 5 --from=iec --padding=10\*[/mono]
.br
\*[mono]$ ls -lh | numfmt --header --field 5 --from=iec --format %10f\*[/mono]
